Third-party components:
  * golang.org/x/net (dns/dnsmessage is used only in a benchmark; idna is
    linked) and golang.org/x/text — BSD-3-Clause, Copyright The Go Authors.
    golang.org/x/net/idna provides the IDNA2008 / UTS-46 hostname
    canonicalization in internal/scope.
  * golang.org/x/sys — BSD-3-Clause, Copyright The Go Authors. Linked on Linux
    only, for SO_REUSEPORT on the sockets of the benchmark's loopback fixture
    (internal/bench/reuseport_linux.go).

These are the only external modules subio links. The DNS wire codec in
internal/dnsfast is first-party rather than a dependency, which is a
deliberate choice measured rather than assumed: against
golang.org/x/net/dns/dnsmessage it builds a query in 42 ns instead of 143 ns
and classifies a response in 59 ns instead of 303 ns, both with zero
allocations (see internal/dnsfast/compare_test.go). The DNS stage resolves
hundreds of thousands of distinct names with no cache to amortize anything, so
that per-name cost is multiplied by the whole workload.

Shared code from a sibling project:
  * Pulse — Copyright (c) 2026 theLabX, MIT License
    (https://github.com/theLabXHQ/pulse). The following were taken from Pulse
    and adapted, under the same ownership and licence:
      - internal/lru (unchanged): bounded LRU used for wildcard verdicts.
      - internal/logx (unchanged): leveled stderr logger.
      - internal/version (adapted): link-time version with a build-info
        fallback; SchemaVersion renamed for subio's ledger.
      - internal/output (adapted): the one-goroutine bounded-channel writer,
        flush-on-empty-queue behaviour, and CSV formula-injection
        neutralization. The record type and text fields are subio's own.
      - internal/cli (adapted): the grouped-help flag registry and the
        listFlag / secondsFlag / sizeFlag value types.
    Pulse's internal/dns was reviewed and deliberately not reused: it is a
    caching, request-coalescing resolver built to look one host up once, which
    is the right design for a prober and the wrong one for mass resolution of
    distinct names.

Provider data:
  subio ships no provider data. Each adapter in internal/sources records the
  provider's own documentation and terms URLs, the limits that documentation
  states, and a reviewed terms state. No provider's data is embedded,
  redistributed or cached by this repository.

Public services queried outside internal/sources:
  `subio relate` can read three public services that are not name providers and
  so have no adapter. None is contacted unless the flag that needs it is given,
  and the limits relate applies to each are subio's own restraint rather than a
  figure the service published:
    * IANA's RDAP bootstrap (data.iana.org/rdap/dns.json) and the registry or
      registrar RDAP servers it names, with -whois. RDAP is the registries' own
      protocol (RFC 9082, RFC 9083), unauthenticated and public.
    * Team Cymru's IP-to-ASN DNS service (origin.asn.cymru.com,
      origin6.asn.cymru.com, asn.cymru.com), with -asn. Cymru asks that bulk
      users take their whois interface instead; relate sends one query per
      target address and one per autonomous system.
    * RIPEstat's announced-prefixes endpoint (stat.ripe.net), with -asn. Free,
      keyless, and asked for one request per autonomous system.
